They matter.

33
00:01:28,080 --> 00:01:35,840
When you meet someone new at work, the conversation, it needs to shift pretty quickly from just social chat to something, well, strategically relevant.

34
00:01:36,020 --> 00:01:36,580
Okay.

35
00:01:36,600 --> 00:01:39,050
Look at the example John and Sarah introducing themselves.

36
00:01:39,550 --> 00:01:41,290
A really key question comes up fast.

37
00:01:41,850 --> 00:01:43,800
Have you been with the company long?

38
00:01:43,780 --> 00:01:44,560
Yeah.

39
00:01:45,400 --> 00:01:47,860
That's a great way to gauge tenure, isn't it?

40
00:01:47,880 --> 00:01:51,870
It tells you instantly about their perspective, their history with the place.

41
00:01:51,990 --> 00:01:52,510
Yeah.

42
00:01:52,530 --> 00:01:55,500
You know, someone there five years versus someone who started last week.

43
00:01:56,060 --> 00:01:57,640
You approach that relationship differently.

44
00:01:57,780 --> 00:01:58,220
Absolutely.

45
00:01:58,360 --> 00:02:07,670
And the dialogues, they really stress identifying departments quickly, marketing, accounting, IT, whatever, not as silos, but as potential collaborators down the line.

46
00:02:07,730 --> 00:02:07,940
Right.

47
00:02:08,010 --> 00:02:09,080
Future points of connection.

48
00:02:09,060 --> 00:02:12,320
And the way they close that first interaction, it's quite telling.

49
00:02:12,340 --> 00:02:14,600
Something like, let's exchange contact information.

50
00:02:14,710 --> 00:02:16,210
It might be helpful to stay connected.

51
00:02:16,610 --> 00:02:19,210
It sounds a little formal, maybe, but effective.

52
00:02:19,470 --> 00:02:20,070
It is effective.

53
00:02:20,110 --> 00:02:22,190
It's understated, but it signals intent.

54
00:02:22,250 --> 00:02:26,000
It basically says, I see potential value here for the future.

55
00:02:26,040 --> 00:02:31,170
It shifts it from just a chat to a potential professional link.

56
00:02:31,330 --> 00:02:40,270
I wonder, though, with Teams, Slack, everything instant now, is that specific phrase about exchanging contacts still necessary?

57
00:02:40,710 --> 00:02:42,700
Or does it sound a bit dated?

58
00:02:42,860 --> 00:02:43,740
That's a fair point.

59
00:02:43,820 --> 00:02:48,570
But I think the phrasing still serves a purpose, even if the action of swapping details happens differently now.

60
00:02:48,750 --> 00:02:49,190
How so?

61
00:02:49,170 --> 00:02:55,880
It's about signaling that intent clearly, saying it might be helpful to stay connected is like diplomatic code.

62
00:02:55,980 --> 00:02:59,520
It means I might need your expertise later and I value that possibility.

63
00:02:59,780 --> 00:03:01,180
It frames it around future benefit.

64
00:03:01,400 --> 00:03:02,480
That's networking, really.

65
00:03:02,840 --> 00:03:03,660
OK, that makes sense.

66
00:03:03,720 --> 00:03:06,590
It's the underlying message and that kind of careful diplomacy.

67
00:03:07,030 --> 00:03:13,460
It seems to run through the small talk examples to things like discussing books or photography or hiking.

68
00:03:13,590 --> 00:03:14,130
Yes.

69
00:03:14,450 --> 00:03:15,450
And that's not just filler.

70
00:03:15,630 --> 00:03:16,610
It's necessary.

71
00:03:16,710 --> 00:03:18,960
It humanizes the professional interaction.

72
00:03:19,480 --> 00:03:21,300
Low stakes ways to build a real connection.

73
00:03:21,660 --> 00:03:23,780
Builds rapport outside the immediate task list.

74
00:03:24,190 --> 00:03:24,690
Precisely.

75
00:03:25,250 --> 00:03:29,090
Small talk is like the lubricant for business interactions.

76
00:03:29,590 --> 00:03:32,380
And this connects directly to understanding company culture.

77
00:03:32,420 --> 00:03:36,190
The sources emphasize engaging in company events, social stuff.

78
00:03:36,220 --> 00:03:39,580
It shows you're integrating, not just there to clock in and out.

79
00:03:39,700 --> 00:03:39,940
Right.

80
00:03:40,260 --> 00:03:47,790
And part of that integration, a big part actually, is engaging with the company's, let's call it the internal operating system.

81
00:03:47,810 --> 00:03:52,900
Meaning being open to giving feedback and maybe more importantly, receiving constructive feedback.

82
00:03:52,920 --> 00:03:54,480
The material really stresses this.

83
00:03:54,940 --> 00:03:55,300
Oh, okay.

84
00:03:55,320 --> 00:04:01,170
So showing you're not just doing your job in a bubble, but you're committed to getting better, to helping the team improve.

85
00:04:01,610 --> 00:04:04,640
That's a core value anywhere that performs well, isn't it?

86
00:04:04,970 --> 00:04:05,750
It absolutely is.

87
00:04:05,770 --> 00:04:07,220
You've got to speak the language of growth.

88
00:04:07,600 --> 00:04:10,900
If all your conversations are just about tasks, you sound transactional.

89
00:04:11,200 --> 00:04:14,650
If you talk improvement, collaboration, you sound strategic.

90
00:04:14,790 --> 00:04:21,800
Okay, so once that rapport is there, we get into the engine room, project management, meetings, logistics.

91
00:04:22,220 --> 00:04:25,100
This is where things can easily get derailed by poor communication.

92
00:04:25,120 --> 00:04:29,210
Let's look at scheduling conflicts first, how the language handles that.

93
00:04:29,340 --> 00:04:32,230
This is where you see those linguistic softeners really shine.

94
00:04:32,530 --> 00:04:35,270
In the example, Mark suggests Monday at 10.

95
00:04:35,770 --> 00:04:36,640
Lisa can't make it.

96
00:04:36,880 --> 00:04:37,080
Right.

97
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:38,440
She doesn't just say no.

98
00:04:38,500 --> 00:04:44,070
She says something like Monday works, but can we do it at 11 a.m. instead?

99
00:04:44,340 --> 00:04:47,240
The but instead combination.

100
00:04:47,300 --> 00:04:48,460
That's the key, isn't it?

101
00:04:48,490 --> 00:04:49,270
It really is.

102
00:04:49,290 --> 00:04:50,650
It validates the first suggestion.

103
00:04:51,030 --> 00:04:52,830
Monday works, so you're not just shutting it down.

104
00:04:53,230 --> 00:04:54,310
Then you propose the change.

105
00:04:54,730 --> 00:04:58,340
It keeps things flexible, professional, but still sets your boundary.

106
00:04:58,360 --> 00:04:59,000
Exactly.

107
00:04:59,500 --> 00:05:01,740
Professionalism extends to setting up the meeting, too.

108
00:05:02,060 --> 00:05:09,390
They agree on outlining steps first, making sure crucial topics like budget and timeline are on the agenda before anything gets sent out.

109
00:05:09,370 --> 00:05:10,430
Preemptive clarity.

110
00:05:11,330 --> 00:05:13,350
Avoids wasting time in the meeting itself.

111
00:05:13,590 --> 00:05:13,810
Right.

112
00:05:14,190 --> 00:05:16,260
And preparation signals competence.

113
00:05:16,280 --> 00:05:18,780
Lesson four in the source material hammers this home.

114
00:05:19,200 --> 00:05:20,340
Those pre-meeting checks.

115
00:05:20,420 --> 00:05:21,800
It's not just showing up on time.

116
00:05:22,060 --> 00:05:22,260
No.

117
00:05:22,880 --> 00:05:28,010
It's about making sure the revised agenda includes that new client feedback section.

118
00:05:28,530 --> 00:05:31,010
It's managing the time precisely okay.

119
00:05:31,380 --> 00:05:34,060
20 minutes for this presentation, then 10 for Q&A.

120
00:05:34,420 --> 00:05:37,380
It's checking the projector, printing reports, all those little things.

121
00:05:37,410 --> 00:05:39,980
Meticulous preparation communicates efficiency.

122
00:05:40,060 --> 00:05:41,330
You look like you've got it together.

123
00:05:41,390 --> 00:05:42,120
Absolutely.

124
00:05:42,620 --> 00:05:48,530
Now let's shift to project communication, especially under pressure like that three month deadline scenario.

125
00:05:48,630 --> 00:05:48,830
Right.

126
00:05:49,010 --> 00:05:49,710
Tight deadlines.

127
00:05:49,750 --> 00:05:51,050
The strategy there is clear.

128
00:05:51,790 --> 00:05:53,810
Prioritize tasks ruthlessly.

129
00:05:54,120 --> 00:05:55,540
Assign responsibilities early.

130
00:05:55,860 --> 00:05:58,980
And crucially, flag resource needs proactively.

131
00:05:59,480 --> 00:06:01,490
Maybe you need additional software, for example.

132
00:06:01,710 --> 00:06:02,430
Mention it up front.

133
00:06:02,450 --> 00:06:03,710
Don't wait until it's a crisis.

134
00:06:03,910 --> 00:06:04,170
Never.

135
00:06:04,570 --> 00:06:07,660
And when you're reporting progress, the language needs to be proactive, too.

136
00:06:07,890 --> 00:06:16,700
If Sarah says the project is on track, she immediately follows up by mentioning potential challenges, mainly technical in the example, but reassures everyone they're handling it.

137
00:06:16,880 --> 00:06:19,250
And the constant refrain is promising regular updates.

138
00:06:19,260 --> 00:06:20,890
That phrase comes up again and again.

139
00:06:20,930 --> 00:06:22,230
Communication is key.

140
00:06:22,650 --> 00:06:25,390
No manager, no stakeholder likes surprises.

141
00:06:25,790 --> 00:06:30,000
So the strategy isn't just about what you report, but how and when.

142
00:06:30,580 --> 00:06:35,020
Reporting issues early, even if you have a fix, builds trust.

143
00:06:35,120 --> 00:06:35,660
Precisely.

144
00:06:35,740 --> 00:06:36,960
It shows you're not hiding things.

145
00:06:37,730 --> 00:06:39,390
The language isn't just status updates.

146
00:06:39,530 --> 00:06:41,090
It's managing expectations.

147
00:06:41,490 --> 00:06:44,830
It demonstrates you're in control even when things get tricky.

148
00:06:45,050 --> 00:06:51,670
Okay, let's tackle some of the really difficult conversations where that mix of clarity and diplomacy is absolutely critical.

149
00:06:52,370 --> 00:06:55,290
Asking for help first, that can be stressful for people.

150
00:06:55,360 --> 00:06:55,780
It can.

151
00:06:56,160 --> 00:07:02,790
Lesson six, with Laura needing help on a budget report, it gives us the perfect template, specificity.

152
00:07:02,910 --> 00:07:03,150
Right.

153
00:07:03,210 --> 00:07:05,450
She doesn't just throw her hands up and say, I'm stuck on the budget.

154
00:07:05,470 --> 00:07:06,930
No, she zooms right in.

155
00:07:07,230 --> 00:07:09,330
It's a classification issue she's struggling with.

156
00:07:09,350 --> 00:07:15,540
And Kevin, the person helping, responds with super specific, actionable language, not vague advice.

157
00:07:15,700 --> 00:07:16,120
Exactly.

158
00:07:16,160 --> 00:07:17,020
It's the detail.

159
00:07:17,300 --> 00:07:20,900
OK, office supplies go under office expenses, travel costs.

160
00:07:21,230 --> 00:07:23,310
Keep those separate from general expenses.

161
00:07:23,290 --> 00:07:26,070
That level of detail makes it immediately useful, doesn't it?

162
00:07:26,350 --> 00:07:26,690
Totally.

163
00:07:27,070 --> 00:07:29,410
The effective way to ask isn't just I need help.

164
00:07:29,550 --> 00:07:33,520
It's more like I'm having trouble categorizing these subsidiary costs.

165
00:07:33,980 --> 00:07:39,980
Specifically, I'm not sure where travel fits versus general operating expenses.

166
00:07:40,800 --> 00:07:43,640
It shows you've tried and it respects your colleague's time.

167
00:07:43,930 --> 00:07:44,400
Good point.

168
00:07:45,110 --> 00:07:47,770
OK, moving to feedback, another high stakes zone.

169
00:07:48,450 --> 00:07:52,650
The sources lay out a pretty structured approach for giving constructive criticism.

170
00:07:52,750 --> 00:07:53,050
Yes.

171
00:07:53,230 --> 00:07:56,640
And the first rule is always start with positive feedback first.

172
00:07:56,760 --> 00:07:58,560
Like your reports are generally well-structured.

173
00:07:58,800 --> 00:07:59,300
Exactly.

174
00:07:59,520 --> 00:08:00,400
Frame it positively.

175
00:08:01,180 --> 00:08:04,610
Then you introduce the area for growth, often using softer language.

176
00:08:05,210 --> 00:08:09,270
Suggesting reports might say lack detail or could use more analysis.

177
00:08:09,350 --> 00:08:12,800
But critically, you follow that immediately with specific examples.

178
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:13,460
You have to.

179
00:08:13,680 --> 00:08:15,280
Otherwise, it's just vague criticism.

180
00:08:15,340 --> 00:08:17,810
The goal is improvement, not making someone feel bad.

181
00:08:18,250 --> 00:08:20,070
Concrete examples make it actionable.

182
00:08:20,310 --> 00:08:21,810
And what about being on the receiving end?

183
00:08:22,050 --> 00:08:24,310
The key there is a proactive stance.

184
00:08:24,730 --> 00:08:25,950
Don't just sit there and nod.

185
00:08:26,390 --> 00:08:27,170
Engage with it.

186
00:08:27,470 --> 00:08:28,470
Ask for clarification.

187
00:08:28,510 --> 00:08:32,600
Okay, could you give me an example of what you mean by more analysis?

188
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:34,960
Shows you're listening and intend to act on it.

189
00:08:35,460 --> 00:08:35,720
Right.

190
00:08:36,180 --> 00:08:38,570
And the scenario suggests proposing next steps.

191
00:08:38,650 --> 00:08:42,590
Like, okay, maybe I should use more charts and graphs for a visual presentation.

192
00:08:42,810 --> 00:08:43,770
It shows ownership.

193
00:08:44,270 --> 00:08:44,730
Makes sense.

194
00:08:45,580 --> 00:08:47,060
All right, negotiation.

195
00:08:47,410 --> 00:08:49,930
This feels like a culmination of many of these skills.

196
00:08:50,170 --> 00:08:50,750
It really does.

197
00:08:50,970 --> 00:08:54,400
It requires a strong but adaptable flow in your language.

198
00:08:54,960 --> 00:09:00,530
The strategy outline starts with understanding the client's budget situation, not just pushing your price.

199
00:09:01,170 --> 00:09:04,010
So understand their constraints first.

200
00:09:04,030 --> 00:09:04,460
Then what?

201
00:09:04,600 --> 00:09:08,250
Then you adjust the scope if needed, maybe offer a slightly smaller package.

202
00:09:08,730 --> 00:09:15,180
Then look for mutual beneficial terms, perhaps a longer contract commitment in exchange for a price adjustment now.

203
00:09:15,360 --> 00:09:16,660
Finding the win-win.

204
00:09:16,920 --> 00:09:17,480
Exactly.

205
00:09:17,920 --> 00:09:23,030
But, and this is crucial, you always stay firm on your quality standards and no compromising there.

206
00:09:23,110 --> 00:09:24,270
So the framing is key.

207
00:09:24,490 --> 00:09:25,330
It's not a battle.

208
00:09:25,510 --> 00:09:27,790
It's collaborative problem solving.

209
00:09:27,770 --> 00:09:28,720
That's the ideal, yes.

210
00:09:29,360 --> 00:09:31,750
You're aiming for long-term value for both sides.

211
00:09:32,270 --> 00:09:37,730
The language should constantly highlight your specific strengths and why a longer relationship is beneficial for everyone.

212
00:09:37,850 --> 00:09:51,570
OK, finally, let's look at how we frame information, especially potentially difficult or sensitive stuff in wider corporate communication, like email etiquette for bad news, a delayed shipment, for instance.

213
00:09:51,590 --> 00:09:51,970
Ah, yes.

214
00:09:52,390 --> 00:09:54,800
The professional email structure is non-negotiable there.

215
00:09:54,860 --> 00:09:56,940
Polite greeting straight to the point.

216
00:09:57,520 --> 00:09:59,990
I am writing regarding order number XYZ.

217
00:10:00,450 --> 00:10:02,690
Clearly state the issue, the delay.

218
00:10:02,990 --> 00:10:06,360
And then the request for an update needs to be polite, right?

219
00:10:06,400 --> 00:10:07,270
Not demanding.

220
00:10:07,370 --> 00:10:07,870
Definitely.

221
00:10:08,430 --> 00:10:10,260
Could you please provide a status update?

222
00:10:10,780 --> 00:10:12,760
It avoids any tone of accusation.

223
00:10:12,910 --> 00:10:17,960
And you mentioned the impact, but objectively, something like this delay is impacting our production schedule.

224
00:10:17,980 --> 00:10:18,480
Exactly.

225
00:10:18,640 --> 00:10:22,520
It provides the necessary context and urgency, but without emotion or blame.

226
00:10:22,900 --> 00:10:25,030
Maintain that professional distance.

227
00:10:25,050 --> 00:10:26,190
Clear, calm, direct.

228
00:10:26,510 --> 00:10:32,250
That same approach, clarity, transparency seems to apply to internal stuff too, like policy changes or finance discussions.

229
00:10:32,390 --> 00:10:32,750
It does.

230
00:10:33,240 --> 00:10:40,320
When reacting to policy updates, maybe new remote work rules or a more casual dress code, the language should tie it back to the strategic purpose.

231
00:10:40,300 --> 00:10:44,200
So connected to improving morale or productivity or whatever the goal is.

232
00:10:44,620 --> 00:10:44,900
Right.

233
00:10:45,380 --> 00:10:47,130
Show you understand the why behind it.

234
00:10:47,310 --> 00:10:48,370
Frame it positively.

235
00:10:48,400 --> 00:10:51,450
And budgets, especially budget cuts.

236
00:10:51,470 --> 00:10:54,090
Lesson 27 really emphasizes honesty and clarity.

237
00:10:54,230 --> 00:10:54,770
Absolutely.

238
00:10:55,190 --> 00:10:57,010
Don't drown people in spreadsheets.

239
00:10:57,030 --> 00:10:57,910
Provide a summary.

240
00:10:58,250 --> 00:11:00,100
Explain the reasons behind the cuts.

241
00:11:00,540 --> 00:11:04,340
That justification is critical for buy-in, or at least understanding.

242
00:11:04,580 --> 00:11:05,620
People need the context.

243
00:11:05,720 --> 00:11:06,140
They do.

244
00:11:06,460 --> 00:11:11,510
And the sources consistently recommend using visuals charts, graphs for finance, or policy stuff.

245
00:11:12,090 --> 00:11:13,530
Make it easier to digest.

246
00:11:13,930 --> 00:11:16,180
Use the tools available to help comprehension.

247
00:11:16,540 --> 00:11:17,720
Don't just rely on words.

248
00:11:18,060 --> 00:11:18,580
Exactly.

249
00:11:18,640 --> 00:11:28,880
Even for the toughest talks, like internal restructuring mentioned in Lesson 34, the focus there is on handling the news with clear, compassionate communication.

250
00:11:29,110 --> 00:11:30,830
Structuring the conversation to reassure people.

251
00:11:31,330 --> 00:11:34,460
Offering concrete support, job search help, severance details.

252
00:11:34,440 --> 00:11:34,820
Yes.

253
00:11:35,200 --> 00:11:36,960
Clarity, empathy, and process.

254
00:11:37,540 --> 00:11:46,390
Ultimately, from that very first introduction right through to wrapping up the day, you know, Lesson 35's simple end-of-day phrase is like, it was productive, thanks, or okay, I'm all set for tomorrow.

255
00:11:46,460 --> 00:11:46,920
See you tomorrow.

256
00:11:47,280 --> 00:11:48,020
Simple stuff.

257
00:11:48,040 --> 00:11:49,560
